## Run it (ROS 2 Jazzy, Gazebo Harmonic, a COCO Lab overlay)

```bash
# once: COCO Lab's packages (coco_lab, gazebo_models, custom_teleop) and this one
cd ~/coco_labs_ws/src/coco-labs && scripts/build_overlay.sh ~/coco_lab_ws
cp -r coco_user_{{NAME}} ~/coco_lab_ws/src_user/ && cd ~/coco_lab_ws
colcon build --base-paths src_user --symlink-install && source install/local_setup.bash

# a level (World Spec -> Gazebo world + Nav2 map and params), once
python tools/m6/gen_level.py worlds/coco_depot_v1.yaml --out ~/gen/coco_depot_v1 --nav-res 0.05 --derivation 5

# one simulator at a time, never --fast
ros2 launch gazebo_models full_world_robo.launch.py gui:=false world:=<the level's .sdf>
ros2 launch custom_teleop arbiter.launch.py initial_mode:=nav use_sim_time:=true
ros2 launch coco_user_{{NAME}} coco_user_{{NAME}}.launch.py \
    map:=~/gen/coco_depot_v1/nav2/coco_depot_v1.yaml params_file:=~/gen/coco_depot_v1/nav2/nav2_params.yaml
```

Then send a goal as with any Nav2 (RViz's "2D Goal Pose", or
`ros2 action send_goal /navigate_to_pose nav2_msgs/action/NavigateToPose ...`).
The STOP and the rest of COCO's safety chain still apply: commands go through
`cmd_vel_arbiter`, which is the only publisher to the wheels.

## What is the same as in the browser, and what is not

- The same file, the same `coco_lab.api` types (filled from the real messages), the same guard
  on its imports, the same output validation and clamping, the same FollowPath rules.
- Not the same world: the costmap is Nav2's, built from Gazebo's LiDAR and the map; time is
  Gazebo's sim time; the robot is simulated with physics (wheel slip, inertia). Results here are
  **STACK** evidence (simulation, real ROS 2), never **MODEL**, and never a real robot's.
- The guard keeps the file portable; on your machine it is not a sandbox (ADR 0012).

